IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`

-- Employment Insurance Act (S.C. 1996, c. 23), Part I, "Rate of Benefits": ss 14, 15, 16 and 17. Layer 1 of piece ei1.
-- Text: the unofficial plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation current to 2026-09-30.
-- Money is a NUMBER of dollars; a rate is a fraction written with %. A percentage or fraction of earnings or benefits is
-- rounded to the nearest dollar by the core's `s 6(2) and s 152.01(5) amount rounded to the nearest dollar`, cited as s 6(2).
-- The maximum yearly insurable earnings is s 4's (the keel) and the published figure is an input, named as the core asks.

§ `Section 14(2) - the weeks by which insurable earnings are divided`

-- src:418 | Regional Rate of Unemployment | Number of Weeks
-- src:419 | not more than 6% | 22
-- src:420 | more than 6% but not more than 7% | 21
-- src:421 | more than 7% but not more than 8% | 20
-- src:422 | more than 8% but not more than 9% | 19
-- src:423 | more than 9% but not more than 10% | 18
-- src:424 | more than 10% but not more than 11% | 17
-- src:425 | more than 11% but not more than 12% | 16
-- src:426 | more than 12% but not more than 13% | 15
-- src:427 | more than 13% | 14
-- Read in nine bands through the core's `Rate band`; "not more than 6%" is the band "6% and under" of the core and
-- "more than 13%" is bands 9 to 12 together.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(2) table (src:418-427)
GIVEN `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(2) the number of weeks by which the insurable earnings in the calculation period are divided` IS
    CONSIDER `the band of the regional rate of unemployment` `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant`
    WHEN `6% and under`                          THEN 22
    WHEN `more than 6% but not more than 7%`     THEN 21
    WHEN `more than 7% but not more than 8%`     THEN 20
    WHEN `more than 8% but not more than 9%`     THEN 19
    WHEN `more than 9% but not more than 10%`    THEN 18
    WHEN `more than 10% but not more than 11%`   THEN 17
    WHEN `more than 11% but not more than 12%`   THEN 16
    WHEN `more than 12% but not more than 13%`   THEN 15
    OTHERWISE 14

§ `Section 14(3) and 14(4) - insurable earnings in the calculation period`

-- src:429 | (3) Insurable earnings in the calculation period are equal to the total of the following amounts established and calculated in accordance with the regulations:
-- src:430 | (a) the claimant’s insurable earnings during the calculation period including those from insurable employment that has not ended but not including any insurable earnings paid or payable to the claimant by reason of lay-off or separation from employment in the qualifying period; and
-- src:431 | (b) the insurable earnings paid or payable to the claimant, during the qualifying period, by reason of lay-off or separation from employment.
-- The amounts are "established and calculated in accordance with the regulations" (reg 23 and reg 24.1, piece er2).
-- Paragraph (a) is the earnings in the calculation period without lay-off or separation pay in the qualifying period;
-- paragraph (b) is that pay.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(3) (src:429-431)
GIVEN -- out of range: reg 23 (piece er2)
      `the claimant's insurable earnings during the calculation period, not including any paid or payable by reason of lay-off or separation from employment in the qualifying period` IS A NUMBER
      -- out of range: reg 24.1 (piece er2)
      `the insurable earnings paid or payable to the claimant, during the qualifying period, by reason of lay-off or separation from employment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(3) the insurable earnings in the calculation period` IS
    `the claimant's insurable earnings during the calculation period, not including any paid or payable by reason of lay-off or separation from employment in the qualifying period`
    PLUS `the insurable earnings paid or payable to the claimant, during the qualifying period, by reason of lay-off or separation from employment`

-- src:433 | (4) The calculation period of a claimant is the number of weeks, whether consecutive or not, determined in accordance with the table set out in subsection (2) by reference to the applicable regional rate of unemployment, in the claimant’s qualifying period for which he or she received the highest insurable earnings.
-- The calculation period is that number of weeks (the table of subsection (2)) "whether consecutive or not" in the
-- qualifying period for which the claimant received the highest insurable earnings. The first rule is the number of weeks;
-- the second picks those weeks out of the insurable earnings of each week of the qualifying period, highest first.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(4) (src:433)
GIVEN `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(4) the number of weeks in the calculation period` IS
    `s 14(2) the number of weeks by which the insurable earnings in the calculation period are divided` `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant`

@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(4) (src:433)
GIVEN `the insurable earnings of each week of the claimant's qualifying period` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
      `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(4) the insurable earnings of the weeks in the calculation period` IS
    sum (take (`s 14(4) the number of weeks in the calculation period` `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant`)
              (reverse (sort `the insurable earnings of each week of the claimant's qualifying period`)))

§ `Section 14(1.1) and 14(2) - weekly insurable earnings`

-- src:412 | (1.1) The maximum weekly insurable earnings is
-- src:413 | (a) $750 if the claimant’s benefit period begins during the years 1997 to 2000; and
-- src:414 | (b) if the claimant’s benefit period begins in a subsequent year, the maximum yearly insurable earnings divided by 52.
-- The years 1997 to 2000 are stated by the text. A benefit period beginning before 1997 is not covered: NOTHING.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(1.1) (src:412-414)
GIVEN `the day on which the claimant's benefit period begins` IS A DATE
      `the maximum yearly insurable earnings for the year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(1.1) the maximum weekly insurable earnings` IS
    BRANCH IF `s 2(1) the year containing` `the day on which the claimant's benefit period begins` BELOW 1997 THEN NOTHING
           IF `s 2(1) the year containing` `the day on which the claimant's benefit period begins` AT MOST 2000 THEN JUST 750
           OTHERWISE JUST (`the maximum yearly insurable earnings for the year` DIVIDED BY 52)

-- src:415 | Weekly insurable earnings
-- src:416 | (2) A claimant’s weekly insurable earnings are their insurable earnings in the calculation period divided by the number of weeks determined in accordance with the following table by reference to the applicable regional rate of unemployment.
-- "Their insurable earnings in the calculation period divided by the number of weeks" of the table, but never more than the
-- maximum weekly insurable earnings of subsection (1.1) (fork F-cap in NOTES.md).
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(2), s 14(1.1) (src:415-416) (src:412)
GIVEN `the claimant's insurable earnings in the calculation period` IS A NUMBER
      `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant` IS A NUMBER
      `the maximum weekly insurable earnings` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(2) the claimant's weekly insurable earnings` IS
    CONSIDER `the maximum weekly insurable earnings`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST ceiling THEN JUST (min ceiling (`the claimant's insurable earnings in the calculation period` DIVIDED BY `s 14(2) the number of weeks by which the insurable earnings in the calculation period are divided` `the regional rate of unemployment that applies to the claimant`))

§ `Section 14(1) - the rate of weekly benefits`

-- src:410 | 14 (1) The rate of weekly benefits payable to a claimant is 55% of their weekly insurable earnings, or 33% of their weekly insurable earnings for the weeks for which the claimant is paid benefits under section 23 in the case where the applicable maximum number of weeks is established under subparagraph 12(3)(b)(ii).
-- 55%, or 33% for the weeks for which the claimant is paid benefits under section 23 where the applicable maximum number
-- of weeks is the 61 weeks of subparagraph 12(3)(b)(ii). The result is rounded to the nearest dollar under s 6(2).
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(1), s 6(2) (src:410)
GIVEN `the claimant's weekly insurable earnings` IS A NUMBER
      `the claimant is paid benefits under section 23 for the week and the applicable maximum number of weeks is established under subparagraph 12(3)(b)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(1) the rate of weekly benefits payable to the claimant` IS
    `s 6(2) and s 152.01(5) amount rounded to the nearest dollar`
        (`the claimant's weekly insurable earnings` TIMES (IF `the claimant is paid benefits under section 23 for the week and the applicable maximum number of weeks is established under subparagraph 12(3)(b)(ii)` THEN 33% ELSE 55%))

-- s 15: [Repealed, 2001, c. 5, s. 5] (src line 435). Nothing to encode.

§ `Section 16 - the family supplement`

-- src:437 | 16 (1) The rate of weekly benefits of a claimant who has one or more dependent children shall be increased by the amount of a family supplement determined in accordance with the regulations if the claimant establishes, in such manner as the Commission may direct, that the claimant meets the prescribed low-income family eligibility criteria.
-- The supplement is "determined in accordance with the regulations" (reg 34(5), piece er2), the "prescribed low-income
-- family eligibility criteria" are the Regulations' (reg 34(3)), and "dependent children" are theirs too: the results are inputs.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 16(1) (src:437)
GIVEN `the claimant has one or more dependent children` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claimant establishes, in the manner the Commission directs, that the claimant meets the prescribed low-income family eligibility criteria` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 16(1) the rate of weekly benefits is increased by the amount of a family supplement` IF
        `the claimant has one or more dependent children`
    AND `the claimant establishes, in the manner the Commission directs, that the claimant meets the prescribed low-income family eligibility criteria`

-- src:443 | (4) The amount of the increase in the claimant’s rate of weekly benefits shall not exceed the prescribed percentage of the claimant’s weekly insurable earnings or, if no percentage is prescribed, 25%.
-- "The prescribed percentage ... or, if no percentage is prescribed, 25%". Reg 34(6) (piece er2) prescribes a
-- percentage by year of establishment; the percentage that applies is an input, NOTHING when none is prescribed.
-- The cap is a percentage of earnings, so it is rounded to the nearest dollar under s 6(2).
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 16(4), s 6(2) (src:443)
GIVEN `the percentage of the claimant's weekly insurable earnings that is prescribed` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the claimant's weekly insurable earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 16(4) the most that the increase in the rate of weekly benefits may be` IS
    `s 6(2) and s 152.01(5) amount rounded to the nearest dollar`
        (`the claimant's weekly insurable earnings` TIMES (fromMaybe (25%) `the percentage of the claimant's weekly insurable earnings that is prescribed`))

-- src:437 | 16 (1) The rate of weekly benefits of a claimant who has one or more dependent children shall be increased by the amount of a family supplement determined in accordance with the regulations if the claimant establishes, in such manner as the Commission may direct, that the claimant meets the prescribed low-income family eligibility criteria.
-- src:438 | Criteria
-- src:439 | (2) The criteria for low-income family eligibility may include criteria that are the same as or similar to the criteria for receiving a Canada child benefit.
-- src:440 | Canada child benefit
-- src:441 | (3) For the purposes of subsection (2), a Canada child benefit is a deemed overpayment under subdivision a.1 of Division E of Part I of the Income Tax Act.
-- src:442 | Maximum increase
-- src:443 | (4) The amount of the increase in the claimant’s rate of weekly benefits shall not exceed the prescribed percentage of the claimant’s weekly insurable earnings or, if no percentage is prescribed, 25%.
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 16(1), s 16(4) (src:437) (src:443)
GIVEN `the rate of weekly benefits increases by a family supplement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amount of the family supplement determined in accordance with the regulations` IS A NUMBER
      `the most that the increase in the rate of weekly benefits may be` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 16(1) and s 16(4) the amount of the increase in the rate of weekly benefits` IS
    IF `the rate of weekly benefits increases by a family supplement`
    THEN min `the amount of the family supplement determined in accordance with the regulations` `the most that the increase in the rate of weekly benefits may be`
    ELSE 0

-- s 16(2) (the criteria may include criteria like those for a Canada child benefit) and s 16(3) (a Canada child benefit
-- is a deemed overpayment under the Income Tax Act) guide the making of the regulations; they are in the coverage table.

§ `Section 17 - the maximum rate of weekly benefits`

-- src:445 | 17 The maximum rate of weekly benefits is 55 % of the maximum yearly insurable earnings divided by 52.
-- 55% of the maximum yearly insurable earnings divided by 52, a percentage of earnings, rounded under s 6(2).
@ref Employment Insurance Act s 17, s 6(2) (src:445)
GIVEN `the maximum yearly insurable earnings for the year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 17 the maximum rate of weekly benefits` IS
    `s 6(2) and s 152.01(5) amount rounded to the nearest dollar` (55% TIMES (`the maximum yearly insurable earnings for the year` DIVIDED BY 52))

@ref Employment Insurance Act s 14(1), s 17 (src:410) (src:445)
GIVEN `the rate of weekly benefits under subsection 14(1)` IS A NUMBER
      `the maximum rate of weekly benefits` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 14(1) and s 17 the rate of weekly benefits, not above the maximum rate` IS
    min `the rate of weekly benefits under subsection 14(1)` `the maximum rate of weekly benefits`
